From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Grische <github@grische.xyz>
Date: Fri, 4 Sep 2026 11:59:48 +0200
Subject: net: lantiq: add a conduit netdev over the xRX500 buffer manager

The transmit path on this SoC is a CPU enqueue into the central buffer
manager, not a private descriptor ring. A transmit DMA channel is bound to
one buffer-manager dequeue port and must point at that port's descriptor
window inside the manager, so it cannot be given a ring in DRAM; length and
the transfer request travel out of band over the manager-to-DMA handshake,
and no completion interrupt exists on such a channel.

Add a network device that transmits accordingly: take a segment out of the
free-segment queue manager, copy the frame in at the fixed data offset,
write one enqueue descriptor with its commit word last, and release the
socket buffer immediately, because the hardware owns the segment from the
commit onwards and recycles it after egress. There is no byte-queue
accounting and no completion poll.

The driver brings up what that path needs and nothing else: the segment
pools out of reserved memory, the CPU enqueue and egress ports, the
dequeue port that drains its datapath port, a flat one-block one-queue
egress path in the traffic manager, the queue-index entry that steers
the endpoint, and the transmit DMA channel. The full buffer-manager
bring-up and the channel programming are present but off by default so
the driver can run beside the buffer manager and DMA drivers, which own
both.

Back pressure is a segment request that answers with the exhausted-pool
marker. Since the channel signals nothing per frame, the stopped queue is
released by a poll of the free-segment count rather than from a receive
context, which would never run for a transmit-only flow. Bringing the
interface down marks the device as going down and lets the poll decline to
rearm, rather than shutting the timer down, which is permanent and would
leave a conduit that came back up with a release poll that could never run.

A writable parameter synthesises exhausted-pool answers so the stop, the
poll and the wake can be exercised without filling the pool. An answer is
spent in the release poll as well as in the segment request, because the
segment request only runs from the transmit path and the transmit path
cannot run while the queue it stopped is stopped. The count is written from
three places and is held under the lock the transmit path already takes
across the segment request.

The transmit header follows the big-endian variant of the layout in
datapath/datapath_api_gswip30.h, lines 279 to 349, of the AVM GPL release.

